{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgNe9cDHRji5EEiqg1PsK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```\nDiagnosis complete — the entire frontend service is missing from disk.\n```\n\n**Root cause:** `docker-compose.yaml` declares `build.context: ./services/frontend`, but `services/` does not exist anywhere in the workspace. Playwright's `webServer` command (`docker compose up --build frontend`, cwd `..`) therefore fails before any browser starts, so the run yields zero test results and the harness reports the synthetic `<startup>` group with an empty failure body. The repair is to create the whole Next.js service under the one declared service root (`services/frontend`), which is also the only path writable per `.claude/settings.json`.\n\n```json\n{\"fix_prompt\":\"# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:<startup>\\n\\n## Diagnosis\\n\\nThe failing set is not an assertion failure — it is a harness *startup* failure. `tests/playwright.config.ts` declares a `webServer` that runs `docker compose up --build frontend` with `cwd: '..'`, waits on `http://127.0.0.1:${port}`, and `docker-compose.yaml` builds that service from `build.context: ./services/frontend`. That directory **does not exist** — there is no `services/` tree in the workspace at all. The compose build aborts, no browser ever launches, the JSON reporter emits no test results, and the harness synthesises the `<startup>` group with an empty failure body.\\n\\nThe repair is therefore to *create* the frontend service from scratch inside the single declared service root `services/frontend` (also the only writable path per `.claude/settings.json`: `Write(services/**)`). Do not touch `tests/`, `docker-compose.yaml`, `docs/`, `true-bdd/`, or `CLAUDE.md` — the port plumbing and build context there are already correct and are not the repair's subject.\\n\\n## Contract the production code must satisfy\\n\\nDerived from `tests/playwright.config.ts`, `docker-compose.yaml` and `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts`:\\n\\n1. `docker compose up --build frontend`, run from the repo root, must build successfully from context `./services/frontend` using the default `Dockerfile` name in that context (compose declares no `dockerfile:` key, so the file must be exactly `services/frontend/Dockerfile`).\\n2. The container must listen on **port 3000 bound to 0.0.0.0** — compose maps `${FRONTEND_PORT}:3000`, and Playwright polls the host port. Binding to `127.0.0.1` inside the container makes the published port unreachable and reproduces the same `<startup>` failure.\\n3. `GET /` must answer with a 2xx (Playwright's `webServer.url` readiness probe accepts 2xx/3xx) and must do so within the 300 s `webServer.timeout`, which covers the *whole* image build plus boot. Keep the build lean.\\n4. The `/` document must contain **exactly one** `<h1>` element whose text is exactly `Hello world`. The spec uses `page.locator('h1')`, which is a strict locator: a second `<h1>` anywhere on the page (a template header, a hero banner) fails with a strict-mode violation, and surrounding whitespace/extra words fail `toHaveText`'s exact comparison.\\n5. `services/frontend/tsconfig.json` must exist and typecheck cleanly — `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` names it as the service's `quality_gate.code` entry for `typescript`.\\n6. Architecture pattern `component-composition`: server components by default; `'use client'` only where hooks or browser APIs are needed. This page needs neither, so **no `'use client'` directive anywhere** in the new code.\\n\\n## Parts to build\\n\\nAll paths are relative to the repo root. Everything lives under `services/frontend/`.\\n\\n### Part 1 — Node package manifest: `services/frontend/package.json`\\n\\nServes: the `<startup>` group (the image cannot be built without it).\\n\\nA private package named e.g. `frontend`. Dependencies: `next`, `react`, `react-dom`. Dev dependencies: `typescript`, `@types/node`, `@types/react`, `@types/react-dom`. Scripts: `dev` (`next dev`), `build` (`next build`), `start` (`next start`), and a `typecheck` (`tsc --noEmit`) for the quality gate.\\n\\nPick a mutually consistent Next/React pair and **verify it actually resolves** by running `npm install` in `services/frontend` before finishing — the npm registry is reachable (`tests/node_modules/.package-lock.json` resolves against `registry.npmjs.org`). Next.js 15.x pairs with React 19.x; if resolution fails for the exact versions you choose, let npm pick the latest compatible set and pin what it resolved. Commit the generated `services/frontend/package-lock.json` so the Docker build is deterministic and can use `npm ci`. If for any reason a lockfile cannot be produced, fall back to `npm install --no-audit --no-fund` in the Dockerfile instead of `npm ci` — never leave a Dockerfile that runs `npm ci` with no lockfile present, because `npm ci` hard-fails in that case and you would re-create the `<startup>` failure.\\n\\nLeaving `node_modules/` installed locally is also what lets the host-side `tsc` quality gate resolve `next`'s types.\\n\\n### Part 2 — TypeScript configuration: `services/frontend/tsconfig.json` (+ `next-env.d.ts`)\\n\\nServes: the `typescript` quality gate declared in `architecture.yaml`; indirectly the `<startup>` group, because `next build` typechecks the app and fails the image build on type errors.\\n\\nStandard Next.js App Router config: `target` ES2017 or later, `lib` including `dom`, `dom.iterable`, `esnext`; `strict: true`; `jsx: \\\"preserve\\\"`; `module`/`moduleResolution` set to the bundler-oriented pair Next expects; `allowJs`, `skipLibCheck`, `esModuleInterop`, `resolveJsonModule`, `isolatedModules`, `incremental`, `noEmit: true`; the `next` plugin in `compilerOptions.plugins`; a `@/*` path alias if you use one; `include` covering `next-env.d.ts`, `**/*.ts`, `**/*.tsx` and `.next/types/**/*.ts`; `exclude` `node_modules`.\\n\\nAlso commit a `services/frontend/next-env.d.ts` containing the two standard triple-slash type references (`next` and `next/image-types/global`). `next build` normally generates this file, but committing it means a bare `tsc --noEmit` on a clean checkout resolves Next's ambient types instead of erroring on missing JSX/module declarations.\\n\\n### Part 3 — Next.js configuration: `services/frontend/next.config.ts`\\n\\nServes: the `<startup>` group.\\n\\nExport a default `NextConfig`. Set `output: 'standalone'` so `next build` emits a self-contained server bundle that the runtime image can run without a full `node_modules` copy — this keeps the image small and the build comfortably inside the 300 s `webServer.timeout`. Keep the rest of the config empty; no rewrites, no basePath (a basePath would break the `page.goto('/')` probe).\\n\\n### Part 4 — App Router root layout: `services/frontend/app/layout.tsx`\\n\\nServes: the `home page greets with Hello world` assertion (a valid HTML document is required for the `h1` locator to resolve) and the `<startup>` group (the App Router refuses to build without a root layout).\\n\\nA default-exported server component taking `{ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }` and rendering `<html lang=\\\"en\\\"><body>{children}</body></html>`. Optionally export `metadata`. **Do not render any heading, nav, header or branding element here** — in particular no `<h1>`, since a second `<h1>` would trip the spec's strict `page.locator('h1')`. No `'use client'`.\\n\\n### Part 5 — Home page: `services/frontend/app/page.tsx`\\n\\nServes: `home page greets with Hello world` (`tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts`) — the substantive assertion behind the set.\\n\\nA default-exported server component for route `/` rendering a single `<h1>` whose only child is the literal text `Hello world`. Write the text as a plain child of the `h1` so the rendered `textContent` is exactly `Hello world` — no nested spans adding whitespace, no interpolation that could introduce a newline, no exclamation mark or extra copy, and no sibling heading elements. A wrapper `<main>` around the `h1` is fine; additional headings are not.\\n\\n### Part 6 — Container image: `services/frontend/Dockerfile`\\n\\nServes: the `<startup>` group — this is the part that actually unblocks the Playwright `webServer`.\\n\\nA multi-stage build on a pinned Node image (an Alpine LTS tag such as `node:22-alpine`; add `libc6-compat` via `apk` if the Next SWC binary complains). Use the **same** base tag in every stage.\\n\\n- *deps stage*: copy `package.json` and `package-lock.json`, run `npm ci` (or the `npm install` fallback from Part 1).\\n- *builder stage*: copy the installed `node_modules` and the source, then run `npm run build`. Set `ENV NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1` so the build makes no outbound telemetry call that could stall a sandboxed build.\\n- *runner stage*: copy the standalone output — `.next/standalone` to the workdir and `.next/static` into `.next/static`. Start with `node server.js`.\\n\\nTwo settings are load-bearing and are the classic cause of a container that builds but never answers the probe:\\n\\n- `ENV PORT=3000` — compose publishes to container port 3000.\\n- `ENV HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0` — the Next standalone `server.js` reads `process.env.HOSTNAME` for its bind address, and Docker otherwise sets `HOSTNAME` to the container ID, so the server binds to an unroutable name and the published port stays dead. Set it explicitly.\\n\\nAlso `EXPOSE 3000`. Only copy a `public/` directory if you actually create one — a `COPY --from=builder /app/public ./public` against a non-existent directory fails the build; the simplest safe choice is to ship no `public/` directory and omit that COPY line entirely. Running as a non-root user is welcome but optional; if you add one, make sure it can read the copied files.\\n\\n### Part 7 — Build-context hygiene: `services/frontend/.dockerignore`\\n\\nServes: the `<startup>` group, by keeping the build inside the 300 s timeout.\\n\\nExclude `node_module